## What I changed

The one genuinely open Tier 1 item was the best finding in the log: **detect-and-revert is an empirical claim and /autonomy published no numbers.** So I counted them — 79 maintainer sessions, 117 commits touching `src/`, **zero rollbacks, zero reverts** (every `grep revert` hit was prose describing the posture). Published with the caveat that makes it honest: a zero is weak evidence when the only detector is the same loop under evaluation. Also noted what it *does* show — corrections here land as forward fixes, not rollbacks, with /raising's "the first version of this section got that wrong" as the worked example.

Then five smaller gloss fixes: LCT on home was self-contradictory ("self-grounding" two words after "grounded in witnessed history") — canon defines LCT by witnessed history and is *silent* on issuance, so I resolved it to self-issued/other-grounded rather than importing a prescription canon doesn't make; witnessed-history clause added to the /projects Web4 card; the Synthon↔coherence definitional loop broken; SNARC's five dimensions glossed including the previously unhedged "Arousal"; and the glossary surfaced in nav ("Glossary" on desktop — "Context & Glossary" would have overflowed an 8-link non-wrapping flex bar; the full label goes in mobile/breadcrumbs/search).

